<head>
  <title>Template syntax — Azox</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Interpolation, event handlers, dynamic attributes and literal text in .azox files." />
</head>

<script>
  import Nav from '../../components/Nav.azox';
  import Footer from '../../components/Footer.azox';
  import DocsNav from '../../components/DocsNav.azox';
  import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.azox';
</script>

<div class="shell">
  <Nav current="docs" />

  <div class="container docs">
    <DocsNav current="/docs/syntax" />

    <main class="doc-body">
      <h1>Template syntax</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A <code>.azox</code> file is HTML with three additions: braces for
        expressions, <code>on:</code> for events, and optional
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> blocks.
        It is not JSX — attributes keep their HTML names, and there is no
        <code>className</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>File structure</h2>

      <CodeBlock label="anatomy"><text><head>
  <!-- optional: copied into the document head -->
  <title>My page</title>
</head>

<script>
  // optional: imports and state
  import { signal } from 'azox/reactivity';
  const count = signal(0);
</script>

<main>
  <!-- the markup, exactly one root element -->
</main></text></CodeBlock>

      <div class="note">
        <p>
          A page needs a single root element in its markup, the way a document
          has one <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. Wrap siblings in a container.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Interpolation</h2>
      <p>Braces evaluate an expression and insert the result as text.</p>

      <CodeBlock label="interpolation"><text><p>Hello, {name()}</p>
<p>Total: {price() * quantity()}</p>
<p>{items().length} items</p></text></CodeBlock>

      <p>
        Values are escaped, so text that looks like markup is shown, not
        interpreted.
      </p>

      <h2>Attributes</h2>
      <p>Quoted values are static; braces make an attribute reactive.</p>

      <CodeBlock label="attributes"><text><div class="card">static</div>

<div class={theme()}>reactive</div>
<img src={user().avatar} alt="Avatar" />
<a href={'/user/' + id()}>Profile</a></text></CodeBlock>

      <p>
        A reactive attribute gets its own effect, so it updates independently
        of everything else on the page.
      </p>

      <h2>Events</h2>
      <p>
        Prefix any DOM event with <code>on:</code>. The expression is used as
        the listener directly.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock label="events"><text><button on:click={() => count.set(count() + 1)}>
  Add one
</button>

<input on:input={(e) => name.set(e.target.value)} />

<form on:submit={handleSubmit}>
  <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form></text></CodeBlock>

      <div class="note">
        <p>
          Handlers can contain object literals and nested braces —
          <code>on:click={'{'}() =&gt; user.set({'{'} name: 'Ada' {'}'}){'}'}</code>
          parses correctly.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Literal text</h2>
      <p>
        To show markup or braces as-is — a code sample, for instance — wrap it
        in <code>&lt;text&gt;</code>. Nothing inside is parsed as markup or
        interpolated.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock label="literal text"><text><pre><text><button on:click={handler}>Not parsed</button></text></pre></text></CodeBlock>

      <p>This documentation is written in Azox and uses it for every snippet.</p>

      <h2>Comments</h2>

      <CodeBlock label="comments"><text><!-- an HTML comment, kept in the output -->

<script>
  // a JavaScript comment, stripped from the output
</script></text></CodeBlock>

      <h2>Reference</h2>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Syntax</th>
              <th>Meaning</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>{'{'}expr{'}'}</code></td>
              <td>Insert the value of an expression, escaped</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>attr={'{'}expr{'}'}</code></td>
              <td>A reactive attribute</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>attr="value"</code></td>
              <td>A static attribute</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>on:event={'{'}fn{'}'}</code></td>
              <td>Attach a DOM event listener</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>&lt;Capitalised /&gt;</code></td>
              <td>A component</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>&lt;slot /&gt;</code></td>
              <td>Where a component places its children</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>&lt;text&gt;…&lt;/text&gt;</code></td>
              <td>Literal content, never parsed</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Two-way inputs</h2>
      <p>
        <code>bind:</code> keeps an element and a signal in step in both
        directions, so you do not write a <code>value=</code> and an
        <code>on:input=</code> that have to agree.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock label="bind:value"><text><script>
  import { signal } from 'azox/reactivity';
  const draft = signal('');
</script>

<input bind:value={draft} />
<p>{draft().length} characters</p></text></CodeBlock>

      <p>
        The element follows the signal, and the signal follows the
        element. Setting the signal from anywhere else updates the input
        too.
      </p>

      <h3>It picks the right property and event</h3>
      <p>
        Written by hand these are easy to get wrong, and wrong quietly.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock label="three kinds of input"><text><!-- binds `checked`, listens for "change" -->
<input type="checkbox" bind:checked={agree} />

<!-- read as a number, so qty() * 2 is 10 and not "52" -->
<input type="number" bind:value={qty} />

<!-- a select listens for "change" -->
<select bind:value={size}>
  <option value="s">Small</option>
  <option value="l">Large</option>
</select></text></CodeBlock>

      <div class="note">
        <p>
          Name the signal, do not call it:
          <code>bind:value={'{'}draft{'}'}</code>, not
          <code>bind:value={'{'}draft(){'}'}</code>. A binding has to
          write back, which a value cannot do — so the called form is
          rejected at build time rather than half working.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>More than one root</h2>
      <p>
        A page or component may have several root elements. They are
        placed directly where the component is used, with nothing wrapped
        around them.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock label="components/Row.azox"><text><script>
  const { label, value } = props();
</script>

<dt>{label}</dt>
<dd>{value}</dd></text></CodeBlock>

      <CodeBlock label="used in a definition list"><text><dl>
  <Row label="Name" value="Ada" />
  <Row label="Role" value="Engineer" />
</dl></text></CodeBlock>

      <p>
        This matters where the parent element restricts what may sit
        inside it: a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> wrapper is invalid in a
        <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code> or a
        <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Importing data</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block can import a
        <code>.json</code> file, for a constant you would rather not
        write out by hand in several places.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock label="reading a version"><text><script>
  import pkg from '../package.json' with { type: 'json' };
</script>

<span>v{pkg.version}</span></text></CodeBlock>

      <p>
        The file is read once during the build. Server rendering
        evaluates against it, and the value is compiled into the page's
        module as a constant rather than imported — the file sits
        outside the build directory and is never deployed, so an import
        would fail in the browser.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <p>
          Only the properties your markup reads are included. Importing
          <code>package.json</code> for a version does not ship the rest
          of the file to every visitor.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="note note-warn">
        <p>
          <strong>Importing a <code>.js</code> module is not
          supported.</strong> It would mean running your project's code
          during the build. Use a <code>.json</code> file for data, and
          <code>azox/reactivity</code> for signals — anything else is
          reported as an error rather than failing silently.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/components">Components</a> — splitting a page up</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/routing">Routing</a> — turning files into URLs</li>
      </ul>
    </main>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</div>
